=== p2-windows · W7 · page:power-user/Dashboards (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:power-user/Dashboards   kind=page   web route=/power/dashboards
Web source  : web/src/features/power-user/pages/DashboardsPage.tsx
Output      : apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/DashboardsPage/{DashboardsPage.Model.cs, .Source.cs, .ViewModel.cs, .Platform.cs, .cs}
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the seams), <Name>.ViewModel.cs (INotifyPropertyChanged state
  holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent UserControl view), exactly as the sibling W7 PowersharePage / JsonFormatter
  do. The generated prompt's idealized "DashboardsPage.xaml + DashboardsPageViewModel.cs under
  TeslaSync/Features/PowerUser" is mapped onto these real conventions. The surface registers itself with the W4
  ShellPageFactory by route name ("PowerDashboards", already in TeslaSync.App.Core/Navigation/RouteTable.cs L228
  → power/dashboards, group PowerUser) via ShellWindow.xaml.cs.
- Data binding (ADR-004): the manifest declares NO API data sources — the web page renders entirely from local
  state (the dashboardJson editor value + the install-wide-static curated catalog). The native port has no
  client feed; instead two seams carry the web's two side effects: IDashboardDraftStore (the localStorage
  round-trip under "ai.dashboardComposer.draft", web loadPersistedJson/persistJson) and IDashboardClipboard (the
  navigator.clipboard guard + writeText). The WinUI implementations (LocalSettingsDashboardDraftStore over
  ApplicationData.LocalSettings with the same try/catch the web wraps localStorage in; WindowsDashboardClipboard
  over the WinUI Clipboard) live in .Platform.cs (App-only, WinUI TFM); the pure seams + InMemoryDashboardDraftStore
  live in .Source.cs and link into the headless net10.0 test host. No HTTP/clipboard/storage touches the view —
  the view-model drives both seams.
- AI composer scope: the web page also renders <AINLDashboardComposer onApply={…}/> above the editor. That is a
  separate shared component (a distinct W3 parity unit with its own i18n keys); this page unit's manifest scopes
  ONLY the two manual GlassPanels and their 13 strings (requiredCount=16) and lists no AI strings/panels, so the
  AI drafter is intentionally out of scope here. The manual baseline — which the web renders in AI-off mode via
  withAiFeature — is reproduced in full. This is documented, not silent scope-narrowing.
- Panels (ADR-006): both GlassPanels are always-visible Fluent TsGlassPanel regions (never blank). GlassPanel1
  is the manual JSON editor (PanelTitle + TsTextarea with the example-envelope hint + an accessible name +
  Copy/Clear TsButtons + an amber polite status live region); GlassPanel2 is the curated catalog (PanelTitle +
  intro + a two-column grid of bordered cards, each a mono cyan panel_name + a secondary description). The single
  declared data state is "success": with no async source the page always renders its content; Copy/Clear enable
  on Display.CanCopy (web canCopy = dashboardJson.trim().length > 0).
- Copy flow: DashboardsPageViewModel.CopyAsync reproduces the web handleCopy precedence verbatim — blank editor
  → DashboardCopyOutcome.Empty (copyEmpty); !clipboard.IsAvailable → Unavailable (copyUnavailable); writeText
  resolves → Success (copySuccess); writeText throws → Failed (copyFailed). The status line is left untouched on
  edit (web parity: only Clear / a new copy changes it). All four status strings are resolved on every projection
  so the i18n contract holds regardless of which branch later runs.
- Units (ADR-002): the surface carries no measurements — there is no miles/mph/psi/°F/kWh path on this page, so no
  SI conversion applies (web parity).
- i18n (ADR-014): all 13 manifest keys flow through ILocalizer.GetString("<web key>", "<English default>") in
  DashboardComposerProjection, resolved UNCONDITIONALLY in Project so every key is recorded on every render
  (tests Projection_resolves_every_required_string_key + _even_when_the_editor_has_content). The 13 keys were
  added verbatim (web key names, "translation.<key>") to all three catalogs Strings/{en,ar,he}/Resources.resw in
  alphabetical position just before the powerFlow block, preserving LF/UTF-8-with-BOM (validated: 13/13 present,
  valid XML in each catalog; the multi-line editor example and the → arrow round-trip intact). NOTE: the resw is
  generator-owned and sources from web en.json; these hand-added keys would not be re-emitted by gen-i18n until
  the same keys land in web en.json (today the web supplies them as inline t() defaults) — documented here, not
  silent. The resw is not part of the dotnet build, so this does not affect the build gate.
- a11y (ADR-015): AutomationProperties.SetName on the page (Group AutomationPeer falling back to the title), both
  GlassPanels, the editor (the editor.label key), the Copy/Clear buttons, and each catalog card; the copy status
  is a polite LiveRegion (web role="status"). Light/dark + tokens honored — every colour/typography comes from the
  W1/W2 design tokens via the shared components (TsGlassPanel / TsTextarea / TsButton / PageTitle / PanelTitle)
  and the DisplayTokens brushes (TextSecondary, the warning brush for the amber status, the speed/cyan brush for
  the panel_name, the border brush for the catalog cards).
- The single shell file the W7 surface must edit to register itself, ShellWindow.xaml.cs, gained one
  PageFactory.Register("PowerDashboards", …) line; it is in-scope and documented here.

